\begin{abstract}
The Shtarkov sum $S(\mathcal{P}) = \sum_{x}\sup_{\theta}P_\theta(x)$ of a parametric family of
probability measures on a finite sample space is the normalising constant of the normalised
maximum-likelihood distribution, and its logarithm is the exact minimax pointwise regret of the
family. We observe that the inner supremum is a tropical (max-plus) sum of log-likelihoods, so that
$S$ is the ordinary mass of the tropicalisation of the class, and we develop two structural
principles for it: a \emph{packing} lower bound, which converts any assignment of models to samples
into a lower bound on $S$, and a \emph{sufficient-statistic} upper bound, which shows that if the
pointwise supremum is dominated by a family of sub-probability measures indexed by the values of a
statistic $T$, then $S \le \lvert\im T\rvert$.

Applying these to the class of binary sources emitted by a fixed deterministic $k$-state automaton
with one free Bernoulli parameter per state, we prove: (i) the class is a probability model and its
likelihood factorises through the vector of per-state emission counts; (ii) the maximum-likelihood
plug-in source dominates every member of the class pointwise, so the tropical envelope is attained;
(iii) consequently $S_n \le \big((n+1)^2\big)^k$ for every $k$-state automaton, i.e.\ the minimax
regret is at most $2k\log(n+1)$, uniformly in the transition structure; (iv) for a single-state
(memoryless) machine the sharpened bound $S_n \le n+1$ holds. On the lower side, the $(n{+}1)$-state
counter machine memorises every word, giving $S_n = 2^n$ exactly and regret $n\log 2$. These combine
into a \textbf{state-budget phase transition}: with $k(n)=\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1$ states the
per-symbol minimax redundancy tends to $0$ for \emph{every} family of automata, while with
$k(n)=n+1$ states it equals $\log 2$---the maximum possible---for every $n\ge 1$.

We also give an entropy-side reformulation: the pointwise supremum equals $e^{-\hat H_M(x)}$, where
$\hat H_M$ is the empirical state-conditional entropy of $x$, so $S_n$ is the partition function of
empirical entropy, and the counting bound becomes a Kraft-type inequality
$\sum_x e^{-\hat H_M(x)} \le (n+1)^{2k}$. Two structural laws round out the theory: the Shtarkov sum
tensorises over independent products of classes with attained envelopes, and it is monotone under
automaton refinement. A pigeonhole consequence bounds the memorisation capacity of automata: a
$k$-state machine can assign probability $1$ to at most $(n+1)^{2k}$ words of length $n$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} tropical semiring, max-plus algebra, Shtarkov sum, normalised maximum
likelihood, minimax regret, universal source coding, finite-state sources, empirical entropy, Kraft
inequality, phase transition.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Regret and the hindsight opponent}

Fix a finite sample space $X$ and a family $\mathcal{P}=\{P_\theta\}_{\theta\in\Theta}$ of
probability measures on $X$. A \emph{universal code} for $\mathcal{P}$ is a single probability
measure $Q$ on $X$; its \emph{pointwise regret} on a sample $x$ is
\[
  R_Q(x) \;=\; \log\frac{\sup_\theta P_\theta(x)}{Q(x)},
\]
the excess codelength (in nats) of $Q$ over the hindsight-optimal member of the family. The minimax
problem $\min_Q\max_x R_Q(x)$ has an exact solution: the minimiser is the \emph{normalised
maximum-likelihood} measure
\[
  Q^\star(x) \;=\; \frac{\sup_\theta P_\theta(x)}{S(\mathcal{P})},\qquad
  S(\mathcal{P}) \;=\; \sum_{x\in X}\sup_\theta P_\theta(x),
\]
and the minimax value is $\log S(\mathcal{P})$. The normalising constant $S(\mathcal{P})$ is the
\textbf{Shtarkov sum} of the class: a single number encoding the entire worst-case behaviour of the
model family.

\subsection{The tropical viewpoint}

Recall the \emph{tropical} (max-plus) semiring
$\mathbb{T}=(\mathbb{R}\cup\{-\infty\},\oplus,\odot)$ with $u\oplus v=\max(u,v)$ and
$u\odot v=u+v$. Then
\[
  \log\sup_\theta P_\theta(x) \;=\; \bigoplus_\theta \log P_\theta(x),
\]
and, since likelihoods multiply along a sequence, each $\log P_\theta(x)$ is itself a tropical
product of per-symbol log-weights. Thus the map $\theta\mapsto\log P_\theta(\cdot)$ turns the model
class into a family of tropical polynomials in the log-parameters; the pointwise supremum
$x\mapsto\sup_\theta P_\theta(x)$ is the (exponentiated) \emph{tropicalisation} of the class; and
the Shtarkov sum is the ordinary total mass of that tropicalisation.

The consequence we exploit is methodological. Tropical objects are piecewise-linear and
combinatorial; their invariants are governed by lattice-point counts rather than by analysis.
Correspondingly, the two principles of Section~\ref{sec:abstract} are purely combinatorial, and the
\emph{only} analytic ingredient in the entire development is the one-dimensional
maximum-likelihood inequality of Section~\ref{sec:ml}, itself a two-atom instance of Gibbs'
inequality.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Two general tools (\S\ref{sec:abstract}): a packing lower bound and a sufficient-statistic
upper bound for arbitrary Shtarkov sums on finite sample spaces.
\item The finite-state class (\S\ref{sec:fsm}): definition, normalisation, count factorisation, and
attainment of the tropical envelope at the maximum-likelihood plug-in.
\item The counting bound (\S\ref{sec:count}): $S_n(M)\le(n+1)^{2k}$ uniformly over $k$-state
automata; the sharpened memoryless bound $S_n\le n+1$.
\item Saturation and the phase transition (\S\ref{sec:phase}): $S_n=2^n$ exactly for the counter
machine, and the resulting dichotomy in the state budget $k(n)$.
\item The entropy bridge (\S\ref{sec:entropy}): $S_n=\sum_x e^{-\hat H_M(x)}$ and a Kraft-type
inequality for empirical entropy.
\item Structural laws (\S\ref{sec:structure}): tensorisation, monotonicity under refinement, and a
memorisation-capacity pigeonhole.
\end{enumerate}

\section{The abstract layer: two principles}\label{sec:abstract}

Throughout this section $X$ is a finite set, $\iota$ a nonempty index set, and $P$ a family of
real-valued weights written $P_i(x)$.

\begin{definition}[Shtarkov sum]
The \emph{Shtarkov sum} of the family $P$ is $S(P)=\sum_{x\in X}\sup_{i\in\iota}P_i(x)$. Its
logarithm is the minimax pointwise regret of the class.
\end{definition}

We always assume $0\le P_i(x)\le 1$. This matters: the index set $\iota$ may be infinite---in our
application it is the parameter cube $[0,1]^k$---so finiteness of the supremum must be supplied.

\begin{lemma}[boundedness]\label{lem:bdd}
If $P_i(x)\le 1$ for all $i,x$, then for each $x$ the set $\{P_i(x):i\in\iota\}$ is bounded above by
$1$. \qed
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[packing]\label{thm:pack}
Let $0\le P_i(x)\le 1$ for all $i,x$. For every finite $A\subseteq X$ and every assignment
$f:X\to\iota$,
\[
  \sum_{a\in A}P_{f(a)}(a) \;\le\; S(P).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For each $a\in A$, $P_{f(a)}(a)\le\sup_i P_i(a)$, the supremum existing by Lemma~\ref{lem:bdd}.
Summing over $A$ gives $\sum_{a\in A}P_{f(a)}(a)\le\sum_{a\in A}\sup_i P_i(a)$. Each term
$\sup_i P_i(x)$ is nonnegative---it dominates $P_{i_0}(x)\ge 0$ for any fixed $i_0$---so enlarging
the index of summation from $A$ to $X$ cannot decrease the sum.
\end{proof}

The interpretation: a class that can \emph{memorise} many samples necessarily has large Shtarkov
sum, hence large regret.

\begin{theorem}[counting]\label{thm:count}
Let $T:X\to Y$ be a statistic and $\{q_y\}_{y\in Y}$ a family of nonnegative weights on $X$ with
$\sum_{x\in X}q_y(x)\le 1$ for each $y$. Suppose the domination hypothesis
$P_i(x)\le q_{T(x)}(x)$ holds for all $i\in\iota$, $x\in X$. Then
$S(P)\le\lvert T(X)\rvert$; in particular $S(P)\le\lvert Y\rvert$ when $Y$ is finite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By domination, $\sup_i P_i(x)\le q_{T(x)}(x)$ pointwise, so $S(P)\le\sum_x q_{T(x)}(x)$. Decompose
over the fibres of $T$:
\[
  \sum_{x\in X}q_{T(x)}(x) \;=\; \sum_{y\in T(X)}\ \sum_{x:\,T(x)=y}q_y(x).
\]
For fixed $y$ the inner sum runs over a subset of $X$ with nonnegative summands, hence is at most
$\sum_{x\in X}q_y(x)\le 1$. There are $\lvert T(X)\rvert$ outer terms.
\end{proof}

Two remarks. The theorem is a \emph{pure counting} statement: the bound is the cardinality of the
image of a statistic. And it is deliberately wasteful: it charges a full unit of mass to each fibre,
whereas the actual mass a fibre carries is typically much smaller.

\begin{corollary}[trivial cap]\label{cor:cap}
If $P_i(x)\le 1$ for all $i,x$, then $S(P)\le\lvert X\rvert$. \qed
\end{corollary}

\section{The one-dimensional maximum-likelihood inequality}\label{sec:ml}

\begin{definition}
For $a,b\in\mathbb{N}$, the \emph{maximum-likelihood Bernoulli parameter} is
$\hth(a,b)=a/(a+b)$ when $a+b>0$, and $\hth(0,0)=0$. It lies in $[0,1]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bernoulli ML inequality]\label{thm:ml}
For all $a,b\in\mathbb{N}$ and $\theta\in[0,1]$,
\[
  \theta^a(1-\theta)^b \;\le\; \hth(a,b)^a\big(1-\hth(a,b)\big)^b .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $a=0$ then $\hth=0$ and the claim is $(1-\theta)^b\le 1$; if $b=0$ then $\hth=1$ and the claim is
$\theta^a\le 1$. Both hold for $\theta\in[0,1]$. Assume $a,b\ge1$; then $t:=\hth=a/(a+b)$ satisfies
$0<t<1$, $1-t=b/(a+b)$, and the right-hand side is strictly positive. If $\theta\in\{0,1\}$ the
left-hand side vanishes; so assume $0<\theta<1$ and take logarithms. The claim becomes
$a(\log\theta-\log t)+b(\log(1-\theta)-\log(1-t))\le 0$. By $\log u\le u-1$ applied to
$u=\theta/t$ and $u=(1-\theta)/(1-t)$,
\[
  a\log\tfrac{\theta}{t}+b\log\tfrac{1-\theta}{1-t}
  \;\le\; a\Big(\tfrac{\theta}{t}-1\Big)+b\Big(\tfrac{1-\theta}{1-t}-1\Big)
  \;=\; \theta(a+b)-a+(1-\theta)(a+b)-b \;=\; 0,
\]
using $t=a/(a+b)$ and $1-t=b/(a+b)$.
\end{proof}

The exact cancellation is the signature of Gibbs' inequality: the first-order term vanishes
precisely because $\hth$ is the empirical mean. Crucially for what follows, the maximiser is a
\emph{function of the counts only}---exactly the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:count}.

\section{Finite-state sources}\label{sec:fsm}

\begin{definition}[automaton]
A \emph{deterministic binary finite-state machine} with $k$ states is a pair $M=(s_0,\delta)$ with
$s_0\in\{1,\dots,k\}$ and $\delta:\{1,\dots,k\}\times\bits\to\{1,\dots,k\}$.
\end{definition}

For $x\in\bits^n$ let $\sigma_0(x)=s_0$ and $\sigma_{i+1}(x)=\delta(\sigma_i(x),x_i)$.

\begin{definition}[source]
For $\theta\in[0,1]^k$ set $w_\theta(s,1)=\theta_s$, $w_\theta(s,0)=1-\theta_s$ and
\[
  P^M_\theta(x) \;=\; \prod_{i=0}^{n-1} w_\theta\big(\sigma_i(x),x_i\big).
\]
The \emph{finite-state class} of $M$ at horizon $n$ is
$\mathcal{P}^M_n=\{P^M_\theta\}_{\theta\in[0,1]^k}$. Clearly $0\le P^M_\theta(x)\le 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[normalisation]\label{thm:norm}
For every $M$, $\theta\in[0,1]^k$, $n$, and start state $s$,
$\sum_{x\in\bits^n}P^{M,s}_\theta(x)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, generalising over the start state. For $n=0$ the empty product is $1$ and there is
one word. For the step, write $x=b\cdot y$ with $b\in\bits$, $y\in\bits^n$. The first factor is
$w_\theta(s,b)$ and the remaining factors are those of $P^{M,\delta(s,b)}_\theta(y)$. Hence
\[
  \sum_x P^{M,s}_\theta(x)=\sum_{b\in\bits}w_\theta(s,b)\sum_y P^{M,\delta(s,b)}_\theta(y)
  =\sum_b w_\theta(s,b)=\theta_s+(1-\theta_s)=1,
\]
by the induction hypothesis at the start state $\delta(s,b)$.
\end{proof}

Generalising over the start state is essential; the statement for $s_0$ alone is not inductive.

\begin{definition}[visit counts]
For $x\in\bits^n$ put $v_{s,b}(x)=\#\{0\le i<n: \sigma_i(x)=s,\ x_i=b\}$, and write
$a_s(x)=v_{s,1}(x)$, $b_s(x)=v_{s,0}(x)$. Each count lies in $\{0,\dots,n\}$ and
$\sum_{s=1}^{k}(a_s(x)+b_s(x))=n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[factorisation]\label{thm:fact}
$\displaystyle P^M_\theta(x)=\prod_{s=1}^{k}\theta_s^{\,a_s(x)}(1-\theta_s)^{\,b_s(x)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Partition the index set $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ into the fibres of $i\mapsto(\sigma_i(x),x_i)$. On the
fibre over $(s,b)$ every factor equals $w_\theta(s,b)$, and the fibre has $v_{s,b}(x)$ elements, so
the sub-product is $w_\theta(s,b)^{v_{s,b}(x)}$. Multiply over the $2k$ pairs and group by state.
\end{proof}

Determinism of $M$ is what makes the counts functions of $x$ alone: this is why the class has a
finite-dimensional sufficient statistic.

\begin{theorem}[maximum-likelihood domination]\label{thm:dom}
Let $\hth(x)\in[0,1]^k$ have coordinates $\hth(x)_s=\hth(a_s(x),b_s(x))$. Then for every
$\theta\in[0,1]^k$ and every $x$,
\[
  P^M_\theta(x) \;\le\; P^M_{\hth(x)}(x),
  \qquad\text{hence}\qquad \sup_\theta P^M_\theta(x)=P^M_{\hth(x)}(x).
\]
The tropical envelope of the class is attained, at the empirical model.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:fact} to both sides and compare factor by factor: for each state,
Theorem~\ref{thm:ml} with $(a,b)=(a_s(x),b_s(x))$ gives the inequality between the corresponding
factors. All factors are nonnegative, so the products compare in the same direction. Attainment
follows since $\hth(x)$ lies in the parameter cube.
\end{proof}

\section{The counting bound}\label{sec:count}

\begin{theorem}[finite-state Shtarkov bound]\label{thm:main}
For every $k$-state automaton $M$ and every $n\ge0$,
\[
  S_n(M) \;:=\; \sum_{x\in\bits^n}\max_{\theta\in[0,1]^k}P^M_\theta(x)
  \;\le\; \big((n+1)^2\big)^{k},
\]
equivalently $\log S_n(M)\le 2k\log(n+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:count} with the statistic
$T(x)=\big(a_s(x),b_s(x)\big)_{s=1}^{k}\in\{0,\dots,n\}^{2k}=:Y$ and, for $y\in Y$, the measure
$q_y:=P^M_{\hth[y]}$ where $\hth[y]_s=\hth(y_{s,1},y_{s,0})$. Domination: $\hth[T(x)]=\hth(x)$, so
Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} gives $P^M_\theta(x)\le q_{T(x)}(x)$ for all $\theta$. Nonnegativity is
immediate. Sub-normalisation: each $q_y$ is a probability measure on $\bits^n$ by
Theorem~\ref{thm:norm}, of total mass exactly $1$. Hence $S_n(M)\le\lvert Y\rvert=(n+1)^{2k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
(i) \emph{Uniformity:} the bound depends on the automaton only through its number of states; no
connectivity or aperiodicity hypothesis is needed. (ii) \emph{Universal cap:} by
Corollary~\ref{cor:cap}, $S_n(M)\le 2^n$ always, so the effective bound is
$\min\{(n+1)^{2k},2^n\}$ and the counting bound is informative exactly when
$2k\log(n+1)<n\log 2$. (iii) \emph{Floor:} by Theorem~\ref{thm:pack} with $A=\bits^n$ and a constant
assignment, $S_n(M)\ge\sum_x P^M_{\theta_0}(x)=1$, so regret is never negative.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[memoryless sharpening]\label{thm:memoryless}
For a single-state machine ($k=1$, the full Bernoulli family) and every $n$,
\[
  S_n(M)\le n+1,\qquad \log S_n(M)\le\log(n+1),\qquad \frac{\log S_n(M)}{n}\le\frac{\log(n+1)}{n}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With one state, $a_1(x)+b_1(x)=n$, so $b_1(x)$ is determined by $a_1(x)$. Run the proof of
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} with the reduced statistic $T(x)=a_1(x)\in\{0,\dots,n\}$ and plug-in
$q_y=P^M_{\hth(y,n-y)}$; the hypotheses are unchanged and $\lvert Y\rvert=n+1$.
\end{proof}

The generic bound would give $(n+1)^2$ here; eliminating the redundant coordinate saves a factor
of $n$. The same elimination applies in general: the counts obey the linear relation
$\sum_s(a_s+b_s)=n$, which the generic bound ignores.

\section{Saturation and the state-budget phase transition}\label{sec:phase}

\begin{definition}[counter machine]
The \emph{counter machine} $C_n$ has $n+1$ states $\{0,\dots,n\}$, initial state $0$, and
$\delta(s,b)=\min(s+1,n)$, independent of $b$. After reading $i$ symbols it is in state
$\min(i,n)$; for $i<n$ it is in state $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[memorisation]\label{lem:mem}
Let $m\le n$ and $z\in\bits^m$. Define $\theta^{(z)}\in[0,1]^{n+1}$ by $\theta^{(z)}_s=z_s$ (as a
$0/1$ value) for $s<m$ and $\theta^{(z)}_s=0$ otherwise. Then $P^{C_n}_{\theta^{(z)}}(z)=1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
At time $i<m\le n$ the machine is in state $i$; the emission weight is $\theta^{(z)}_i=1$ if
$z_i=1$, and $1-\theta^{(z)}_i=1$ if $z_i=0$. Every factor is $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[saturation]\label{thm:sat}
For $m\le n$, $S_m(C_n)=2^m$. In particular $S_n(C_n)=2^n$ and the minimax regret of the
$(n{+}1)$-state counter class at horizon $n$ is exactly $n\log 2$: no compression is possible in the
worst case.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Lower bound: Theorem~\ref{thm:pack} with $A=\bits^m$ and $f(z)=\theta^{(z)}$; by
Lemma~\ref{lem:mem} each term is $1$, so $S_m(C_n)\ge 2^m$. Upper bound: Corollary~\ref{cor:cap}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{regret} of $M$ at horizon $n$ is $\rho(M,n)=\log S_n(M)\ge0$; the \emph{regret rate} is
$\rho(M,n)/n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[vanishing regret rate]\label{thm:rate}
Let $k:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ and let $M_n$ be any automaton with $k(n)$ states. If
$k(n)\log(n+1)/n\to0$, then $\rho(M_n,n)/n\to0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, $0\le\rho(M_n,n)/n\le 2k(n)\log(n+1)/n$; both bounding sequences tend to
$0$, so squeeze.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sqrt}
The budget $k(n)=\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1$ satisfies the hypothesis of Theorem~\ref{thm:rate}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $n\ge1$, $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1\le2\sqrt n$, so
\[
  \frac{(\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1)\log(n+1)}{n}\le\frac{2\log(n+1)}{\sqrt n}
  \le\frac{4\log(n+1)}{\sqrt{n+1}},
\]
using $\sqrt{n+1}\le2\sqrt n$. Since $\log y/\sqrt y\to0$ (substitute $y=t^2$ and use
$\log t/t\to0$), the right-hand side tends to $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[state-budget phase transition]\label{thm:phase}
Both of the following hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Vanishing side.) For \emph{every} family of automata $M_n$ with $\lfloor\sqrt n\rfloor+1$
states, $\rho(M_n,n)/n\to0$.
\item (Saturated side.) For the counter family with $n+1$ states, $\rho(C_n,n)/n=\log2$ for every
$n\ge1$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:rate} with Lemma~\ref{lem:sqrt}; (2) is Theorem~\ref{thm:sat}, since
$\rho(C_n,n)=\log 2^n=n\log2$.
\end{proof}

This is a dichotomy in the strongest sense, not a matter of constants. On the sub-linear side the
per-symbol overhead is asymptotically free, uniformly over all automata with that many states. On
the linear side it is exactly maximal: the class has degenerated into a lookup table. The threshold
sits at $k(n)\asymp n/\log n$, where $2k(n)\log(n+1)$ crosses $n\log2$.

\section{The entropy bridge}\label{sec:entropy}

Write $h(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)$ for the binary entropy in nats, with $h(0)=h(1)=0$; then
$0\le h\le\log2$ on $[0,1]$, maximal at $p=1/2$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mlent}
For all $a,b\in\mathbb{N}$,
$\ \hth(a,b)^a(1-\hth(a,b))^b=\exp\big(-(a+b)h(\hth(a,b))\big)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $a=b=0$ both sides are $1$. Otherwise set $t=a/(a+b)$; then $a=(a+b)t$ and $b=(a+b)(1-t)$, so
the logarithm of the left side is $a\log t+b\log(1-t)=(a+b)(t\log t+(1-t)\log(1-t))=-(a+b)h(t)$.
Each factor is strictly positive whenever its exponent is nonzero, so the logarithms are legitimate.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[empirical entropy]
The \emph{empirical state-conditional entropy} of $x$ relative to $M$ is
\[
  \hat H_M(x) \;=\; \sum_{s=1}^{k}\big(a_s(x)+b_s(x)\big)\,h\big(\hth(a_s(x),b_s(x))\big).
\]
It is the ideal codelength of $x$ under a state-conditional code whose per-state biases are fitted
to $x$ after the fact.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[partition function]\label{thm:partition}
For every $M$, $n$, and $x$, $\ \max_\theta P^M_\theta(x)=e^{-\hat H_M(x)}$, and hence
\[
  S_n(M) \;=\; \sum_{x\in\bits^n}e^{-\hat H_M(x)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:dom} the maximum is $P^M_{\hth(x)}(x)$, which by Theorem~\ref{thm:fact}
factorises over states; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:mlent} in each state and multiply, the exponents
adding to $-\hat H_M(x)$. Sum over $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Kraft-type inequality for empirical entropy]\label{thm:kraft}
For every $k$-state automaton and every $n$,
$\ \sum_{x\in\bits^n}e^{-\hat H_M(x)}\le\big((n+1)^2\big)^{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:partition} and~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

Kraft's inequality states that the codeword lengths of a prefix code satisfy
$\sum_x e^{-\ell(x)}\le1$. The quantities $\hat H_M(x)$ are \emph{not} codeword lengths of any
single code---they are obtained by refitting the model to each $x$---and
Theorem~\ref{thm:kraft} quantifies exactly how much they may violate Kraft: by at most
$2k\log(n+1)$ nats. This is the model-cost term of minimum description length, derived purely by
counting.

\begin{proposition}
$0\le\hat H_M(x)\le n\log2$ for every $x$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity is termwise. For the upper bound, $h\le\log2$ termwise gives
$\hat H_M(x)\le\log2\sum_s(a_s+b_s)=n\log2$, since the visit counts partition $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural laws}\label{sec:structure}

\begin{theorem}[tensorisation]\label{thm:tensor}
Let $\{P_i\}_{i\in\iota}$ on $X$ and $\{Q_j\}_{j\in\kappa}$ on $Y$ take values in $[0,1]$ and have
attained envelopes: there exist $f_P:X\to\iota$, $f_Q:Y\to\kappa$ with $P_i(x)\le P_{f_P(x)}(x)$ and
$Q_j(y)\le Q_{f_Q(y)}(y)$. For the product class $(P\otimes Q)_{(i,j)}(x,y)=P_i(x)Q_j(y)$,
\[
  S(P\otimes Q) \;=\; S(P)\cdot S(Q).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $(x,y)$ the supremum is attained at $(f_P(x),f_Q(y))$: indeed
$P_i(x)Q_j(y)\le P_{f_P(x)}(x)Q_j(y)\le P_{f_P(x)}(x)Q_{f_Q(y)}(y)$ by nonnegativity. Hence the
pointwise supremum of the product class factorises, and the double sum splits.
\end{proof}

Taking logarithms: minimax regret is additive over independent components. This is why regret
behaves like a dimension count; it is also multiplicativity of tropicalisation under products.

\begin{definition}[simulation]
$M'$ with $k'$ states \emph{simulates} $M$ with $k$ states if there is
$\pi:\{1,\dots,k'\}\to\{1,\dots,k\}$ with $\pi(s'_0)=s_0$ and $\pi(\delta'(s,b))=\delta(\pi(s),b)$
for all $s,b$. Then $M'$ is a \emph{refinement} of $M$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:sim}
If $M'$ simulates $M$ via $\pi$, then $\pi(\sigma'_i(x))=\sigma_i(x)$ for all $i,x$, and
$P^M_\theta=P^{M'}_{\theta\circ\pi}$ for every $\theta\in[0,1]^k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is induction on $i$: base case $\pi(s'_0)=s_0$, step by the intertwining property.
The two defining products then have equal factors termwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[monotonicity under refinement]
If $M'$ simulates $M$, then $S_n(M)\le S_n(M')$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:sim} every member of $\mathcal{P}^M_n$ belongs to $\mathcal{P}^{M'}_n$, so at
every word the supremum over the larger class dominates; sum over words.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[memorisation capacity]\label{thm:capacity}
Let $M$ have $k$ states and $A\subseteq\bits^n$ be such that for each $x\in A$ there is
$\theta^{(x)}$ with $P^M_{\theta^{(x)}}(x)\ge1$. Then $\lvert A\rvert\le\big((n+1)^2\big)^{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:pack} with $f(x)=\theta^{(x)}$,
$\lvert A\rvert=\sum_{x\in A}1\le\sum_{x\in A}P^M_{\theta^{(x)}}(x)\le S_n(M)$, and
$S_n(M)\le(n+1)^{2k}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[automata cannot memorise]
If $(n+1)^{2k}<2^n$ then there is $x\in\bits^n$ with $P^M_\theta(x)<1$ for \emph{every}
$\theta\in[0,1]^k$. In particular this holds for a single-state machine whenever $n\ge6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If every word were memorisable, Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} with $A=\bits^n$ would give
$2^n\le(n+1)^{2k}$. The numerical claim $(n+1)^2<2^n$ for $n\ge6$ follows by induction from
$7^2=49<64=2^6$, since doubling outpaces $\big(\tfrac{n+2}{n+1}\big)^2\le2$.
\end{proof}

A $k$-state automaton, however cleverly wired and however finely its $k$ real parameters are tuned,
has memorisation capacity at most $2k\log(n+1)$ nats. The uncountable parameter space does not
help: it is the finiteness of the sufficient statistic, not the cardinality of the parameter set,
that bounds capacity.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A: exact Shtarkov sum by count enumeration.}
By Theorems~\ref{thm:dom} and~\ref{thm:fact} the maximised likelihood of $x$ depends only on the
count vector $c(x)$, so
\[
  S_n(M)=\sum_{c}N(c)\prod_{s=1}^{k}\hth(a_s,b_s)^{a_s}\big(1-\hth(a_s,b_s)\big)^{b_s},
\]
where $N(c)$ is the number of words with count vector $c$. The multiplicities can be accumulated by
a dynamic program over $(\text{time},\text{state},\text{counts})$, or by direct enumeration of the
$2^n$ words. Direct enumeration costs $\Theta(2^n n)$; the dynamic program costs
$O(n\,k\,\lvert\mathcal{C}_n\rvert)$ with $\mathcal{C}_n$ the reachable count set, polynomial in $n$
for fixed $k$---an exponential speed-up possible only because the class has a sufficient statistic.

\paragraph{Algorithm B: empirical entropy.}
Run the automaton on a word, tally the $2k$ counts, return $\sum_s(a_s+b_s)h(a_s/(a_s+b_s))$; cost
$\Theta(n+k)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:partition} this is exactly $-\log$ of the maximised likelihood.

\paragraph{Algorithm C: packing certificate.}
Given a set $A$ of words and a parameter vector for each, evaluate
$\sum_{a\in A}P_{\theta^{(a)}}(a)$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:pack} this is a certified lower bound on
$S_n(M)$. For the counter machine with $\theta^{(z)}$ as in Lemma~\ref{lem:mem} it equals $2^n$,
proving saturation numerically.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{What the bound really is.}
The classical statement ``a $d$-parameter family has minimax regret $\tfrac{d}{2}\log n+O(1)$'' is
usually derived by Laplace approximation of $\int\sqrt{\det I(\theta)}\,d\theta$. The development
above shows that a bound of the right \emph{logarithmic order} requires no analysis at all: it is
the cardinality of the image of a sufficient statistic. Analysis contributes only the constant
$\tfrac12$ per parameter, by replacing ``one unit of mass per statistic value'' with ``the actual
fibre mass'', which is $\Theta(n^{-1/2})$ per parameter because a binomial fibre has Gaussian width
$\sqrt n$.

\paragraph{Why the state count and nothing else.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is insensitive to the transition structure because the counting bound uses
only the \emph{range} of the statistic, contained in a box $\{0,\dots,n\}^{2k}$ for any wiring.
Structure enters only through constraints that shrink the range: the universal relation
$\sum_s(a_s+b_s)=n$ (worth one factor of $n$, as in Theorem~\ref{thm:memoryless}), and the
Kirchhoff-type flow relations forced by the transition graph, since state visits are edge
frequencies of a closed walk. Exploiting these replaces the box by a lattice polytope and
$(n+1)^{2k}$ by the corresponding Ehrhart count.

\paragraph{Positioning of the phase transition.}
The classical intuition ``more model, more overfitting'' is here made quantitative and, in a sense,
reassuring: the overhead of an unboundedly growing model class can still be asymptotically
negligible per symbol, provided the growth is sub-linear in $n/\log n$. Only at linear growth does
the class become a memory.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
(i) The bound is off by a square root per parameter, giving $2k\log n$ where the truth is
$\tfrac{k}{2}\log n$. (ii) The lower bound is proved here only for the counter machine; a matching
$\Omega(n^{k/2})$ for a general strongly connected machine requires a packing construction on the
lattice of realisable count vectors. (iii) The alphabet is binary; the extension to alphabets of
size $q$ should replace $(n+1)^2$ by $(n+1)^{q}$ per state and $h$ by the $q$-ary entropy, with no
conceptual change.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[the $\tfrac{k}{2}\log n$ law by tropical counting]
For every fixed $k\ge1$ there are constants $0<c_k\le C_k$ with
$c_k n^{k/2}\le S_n(M)\le C_k n^{k/2}$ for every strongly connected $k$-state machine $M$ and all
$n\ge k$.
\end{conjecture}

The sufficient statistic used in the counting bound is wasteful by exactly one square root per
state: the bound charges $(n+1)^2$ per state, while the mass the plug-in distribution concentrates
on one fibre is $\Theta(n^{-1/2})$ per state---the Gaussian width of a binomial fibre. Replacing
``one unit per statistic value'' by ``the actual fibre mass'' should convert the counting bound into
a sharp estimate with no machinery beyond a Stirling bound for the central binomial coefficient. The
three ingredients---fibre decomposition, plug-in domination, packing---are in place; the missing
step is a bulk lower bound $\binom{n}{j}(j/n)^j((n-j)/n)^{n-j}\ge c/\sqrt n$. Numerics pin the
constants: $S_n\approx\sqrt{\pi n/2}$ for $k=1$ and $S_n\approx n$ for $k=2$.

\begin{conjecture}[the image of the count statistic is a flow polytope]
For a strongly connected $k$-state machine and $n\ge k^2$, the image of the count statistic is
exactly the set of vectors $\big((a_s,b_s)\big)_s$ with $\sum_s(a_s+b_s)=n$ realisable as
Eulerian-type flows on the transition graph, and its cardinality is $\Theta(n^{2k-1})$.
\end{conjecture}

The counting bound ignores the linear constraint $\sum_s(a_s+b_s)=n$ and the Kirchhoff/flow
constraints of the transition graph: state visits are edge frequencies of a closed walk, so the
statistic lands on the lattice points of a flow polytope. Making this exact would give the first
structure-sensitive Shtarkov bound and would immediately shave a power of $n$ off the generic
estimate.

Beyond these: a matching lower bound for general strongly connected machines via packing on the
realisable count lattice; the $q$-ary extension; a treatment of context-tree classes as a limit of
finite-state classes ordered by the refinement relation, where monotonicity gives a coherent notion
of a Shtarkov sum for the whole tree; and an operational reading of the state-budget phase
transition for adaptive dictionary coders.

